  { id:'opqb-t12-498', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'**Pars plana vitrectomy** is **NOT** indicated in:',
    options:['long standing vitreous hemorrhage','Tractional retinal detachment','central serous retinopathy','intraocular foreign body'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Three of these four are problems inside the vitreous cavity. The fourth is a leak under the macula, and you cannot reach it with a vitrectomy probe.**\n\n**What the module lists as an indication, in its own words:**\n\n| Option | Where the module puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **Long standing vitreous hemorrhage** | `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy`, under *Indications for vitreoretinal surgery*, prints **\"Severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage\"** as the first of four. *Persistent* is the operative word — fresh blood is watched, blood that will not clear is removed. |\n| **Tractional retinal detachment** | `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 15 gives TRD exactly one operation: **\"Vitrectomy & Membrane Peeling: Surgeons remove the vitreous and carefully peel away FIBROVASCULAR MEMBRANES or scar tissue that is pulling on the retina.\"** `L19.1` lists the same case from the diabetic side as **\"Progressive proliferation despite laser therapy\"** and **\"Retinal detachment involving macula\"**. |\n| **Intraocular foreign body** | `L25) Trauma I` slide 52 stops at the work-up — *\"The presence of an intraocular foreign body (IOFB) suggests globe penetration\"*, then CT to locate it and **\"DO NOT DO MRI SCAN IF A METALLIC IOFB IS SUSPECTED\"**. That a posterior-segment IOFB is retrieved **through a pars plana vitrectomy**, because that is the only route to the vitreous cavity and the retina behind it, is *not taken from the course material* — the deck names the diagnosis and the imaging and never names the operation. |\n\n**Why central serous retinopathy is the odd one out.** It is a **serous (exudative) detachment of the neurosensory retina at the macula, caused by a leak from the choroid through a defective retinal pigment epithelium** — no tear, no traction, and no vitreous pathology at all *(not taken from the course material; see the measured absence below)*. The vitreous is not the problem, so removing it changes nothing. The module's own logic says the same thing from the other direction: `L20` slide 16 handles the exudative detachment and opens **\"SURGERY IS RARELY THE FIRST STEP; the focus is on stopping fluid leakage\"** — medical therapy, and anti-VEGF injections for leaking vessels. Central serous retinopathy is the mildest member of that family and most cases resolve on observation alone *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**⚠️ How the absence was measured, rather than assumed.** Grepping `central serous`, `serous retinopathy`, `CSCR` and `CSR` case-insensitively across all 29 lecture caches in `content\\ophtho\\lectures\\` and all 12 cached book chapters in `content\\ophtho\\book\\` returns **exactly two hits, and neither teaches it**: `L20`'s own closing NOT-COVERED list records **\"CENTRAL SEROUS CHORIORETINOPATHY … not covered\"**, and `L23) Ocular manifestations of systemic diseases` mentions only that there is *\"an increased incidence of central serous chorioretinopathy and uveal effusions in pregnant versus nonpregnant women\"*. The condition is named in this module and never described, so its pathology above is supplied and tagged.\n\n**The rule worth carrying out of this question:** vitrectomy earns its place when the **vitreous itself** is the obstacle — opaque with blood, pulling on the retina, or holding a foreign body. A subretinal leak is on the wrong side of the retina for it.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Vitrectomy is indicated where the vitreous is the problem — non-clearing vitreous haemorrhage, tractional detachment, an intraocular foreign body; central serous retinopathy is a subretinal leak and is not a vitrectomy case.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.79 (Q498), key p.81' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-499', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'**Vitrectomy** is carried out in cases of:',
    options:['Non-resolving vitreous hemorrhage.','Non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy.','Choroidal melanoma.','High myopia.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The same list as `opqb-t12-498`, asked from the positive side.** `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` prints four *Indications for vitreoretinal surgery*: **severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage · dense, persistent premacular haemorrhage · progressive proliferation despite laser therapy · retinal detachment involving macula.** Option A is the first of them, and the word **non-resolving** is what makes it an indication: blood in the vitreous is given time to clear on its own, and it is the failure to clear that brings the patient to theatre.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy.** | ⚠️ **The trap is the single word \"Non-\".** `L19.1` reserves surgery for the *proliferative* end of the disease and even then only after laser has failed; in NPDR there is no new vessel, no fibrovascular membrane and no traction. The deck's own treatment for the one thing NPDR does threaten — clinically significant macular oedema — is **grid or focal photocoagulation and intravitreal anti-VEGF injection**, not vitrectomy. |\n| **Choroidal melanoma.** | `L24) Ocular tumors.` slide 17 prints the entire treatment list for an intraocular choroidal melanoma: **\"Photocoagulation · Radioactive plaques · External beam irradiation · Enucleation\"**, with exenteration for extraocular extension and palliation for metastasis. **Vitrectomy is not on it.** Nor could it be — the tumour is a choroidal mass under the retina, so cutting into the eye would neither remove it nor debulk it, and would risk seeding a malignancy along the sclerotomies *(the seeding risk is not taken from the course material; the treatment list is)*. |\n| **High myopia.** | A refractive error, not a vitreous disease. `L7,8) Refractive errors` slide 46 lists its complications — chorioretinal degenerations, **retinal tears and RRD**, posterior staphyloma, complicated cataract, an association with open-angle glaucoma — and the treatment it gives for myopia itself is spectacles, contact lenses or refractive surgery on the **cornea or the lens**. A myope who goes on to detach a retina is operated for the **detachment**, not for the myopia. |\n\n**⚠️ Read A and B together and the question resolves itself:** both are diabetic-eye options, and the bank is testing whether you know that vitrectomy is a **late, salvage** operation in diabetes — after the bleeding, after the proliferation, after laser. Compare `opqb-t13-544`, where a diabetic with disc neovascularisation and an **attached** retina is keyed to panretinal photocoagulation, not to vitrectomy.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A vitreous haemorrhage that will not clear is the classic vitrectomy indication; non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy, choroidal melanoma and high myopia are each treated by something else.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.79 (Q499), key p.81' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-500', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'Regarding **posterior vitreous detachment**:',
    options:['It is more common in hypermetropies.','It is due to separation of the vitreous from the choroid.','It always leads to retinal detachment.','It is a physiological aging process'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**PVD is what the vitreous does to everybody who lives long enough. The book files it under ageing, not under disease.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.17 prints it inside the section headed **\"Age-Related Changes in the Eye and its Adnexa\"**, among the *intraocular* involutional changes, in one sentence that answers three of these four options at once:\n\n> \"The vitreous humour degenerates and condenses (**vitreous syneresis**) which predisposes to the separation of the vitreous from its attachments **to the retina and optic disc**, called a posterior vitreous detachment (**PVD**). This **can predispose** the patient to retinal traction, retinal tears, and, **in the worst-case scenario**, retinal detachment.\"\n\n**Option by option:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **It is more common in hypermetropies.** | **False — it is the myope who gets it early.** `L7,8) Refractive errors` slide 46 lists **retinal tears** and **RRD** among the peripheral complications of myopia, and `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 6 names **\"high myopia (nearsightedness)\"** alongside ageing as a cause of rhegmatogenous detachment. The long globe stretches and thins the peripheral retina and liquefies the gel sooner, so a myope reaches PVD a decade or more before an emmetrope *(the mechanism is not taken from the course material; myopia as the risk factor is `L20` slide 6 and `L7,8` slide 46)*. The bank's own Q502 two numbers later makes its patient **highly myopic** for exactly this reason. |\n| **It is due to separation of the vitreous from the choroid.** | **False, and it is an anatomical impossibility** — the retina lies between the two, so the vitreous never touches the choroid. The book names the attachments the vitreous actually gives up: **\"its attachments to the retina and optic disc\"**. |\n| **It always leads to retinal detachment.** | **False.** The book's own hedging is the evidence: **\"can predispose\"**, and detachment is **\"the worst-case scenario\"**. Most posterior vitreous detachments are uneventful; the danger is confined to the places where the gel is stuck hard enough to tear the retina on its way off, which is why Q504 keys a dilated fundus examination rather than reassurance. |\n| **It is a physiological aging process** | **TRUE — the printed key.** The book classifies it as **involutional**, i.e. a normal change of age, in the same list as nuclear sclerosis of the lens and a smaller, less reactive pupil. |\n\n**⚠️ A correction worth recording, because the module contradicts itself about whether PVD is taught at all.** `L20`'s closing NOT-COVERED list states that **\"POSTERIOR VITREOUS DETACHMENT (PVD) as a named entity, and Weiss ring\"** are absent — *\"The deck says only 'aging (vitreous shrinkage)'\"* — and `L31,32) Acute visual Loss` records the same gap. **Both statements are true of their own decks and neither is true of the module.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17 names PVD, defines it and gives its mechanism; p.41 lists **PVD** first among the causes of **flashes of light (photopsia)**; p.42 lists **\"Posterior vitreous detachment (PVD)\"** first among the causes of **floaters**. A deck's NOT-COVERED list is scoped to that deck. Measured: `posterior vitreous` returns three hits across all lecture and book caches — one inside `L20`'s own negatives list, two in `ophthalmology.pdf` chapter 1.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'PVD is a physiological involutional change — the vitreous separating from the retina and optic disc after syneresis; it is commoner in myopes, never involves the choroid, and only sometimes causes a tear.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.79 (Q500), key p.81' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-501', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'On performing **red reflex**, the left eye shows **black reflex**, what is the possible cause?',
    options:['Retinal detachment','Deep vitreous hemorrhage','Endophthalmitis','B&C'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"*(⚠️ **THIS QUESTION CARRIES A FIGURE** — a flash close-up of a child's two eyes, printed between the stem and option A. **No crop was cut**: the page, column and 400 dpi ink bounds are recorded in `gg-t12.array.js`, together with the measurement that no printed rule sits beneath the photograph and that the option line \"A. Retinal detachment\" begins 28 px below it. The stem is answerable **without** the image, because the bank has already put the finding into words — the picture illustrates the black reflex rather than asking you to find it. ⚠️ Option D is printed exactly as **\"B&C\"**.)*\n\n**`L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slides 49–52 are a four-colour key, and this question is one line of it.**\n\n| Reflex seen | What it means (`L3,4` slides 51–52) |\n|---|---|\n| **Normal red** | *\"Light reflected off the fundus … Normal red reflex is not interrupted by shadows, and is evidence that the cornea, anterior chamber, lens, and vitreous are clear\"* (slide 49). |\n| **Black shadows within a red reflex** | *\"Opacities in the media (e.g., corneal scar, cataract, vitreous haemorrhage) appear as **black shadows** and can be best seen when the pupil has been dilated\"* (slide 50). |\n| **ABSENT / BLACK** | *\"Mature cataract\"* · **\"Dense vitreous hemorrhage (Black reflex)\"** (slide 51). |\n| **GREY** | **\"Retinal detachment\"** (slide 51). |\n| **YELLOW / WHITE** | *\"Retinoblastoma\"* · **\"Pus in the vitreous (endophthalmitis)\"** (slide 52). |\n\nThe deck prints the words **\"Black reflex\"** exactly once, in brackets after **dense vitreous haemorrhage**, and that is the printed key.\n\n**Why the other three fail, and why option D is the real distractor:**\n\n- **Retinal detachment** gives a **grey** reflex, not a black one — `L3,4` slide 51 puts it on a separate line for that reason, and the corpus already keys the point at `opqb-t1-48` (*\"A grey reflex instead of a red reflex is typical of…\"* → retinal detachment) and again at `opqb-t1-61`. The detached retina is still a reflecting surface, just the wrong colour and in the wrong plane; blood absorbs the light instead of reflecting it.\n- **Endophthalmitis** gives a **yellowish or white** reflex — slide 52, *\"Pus in the vitreous\"* — which is precisely what this same bank keys three questions later at Q512, and what the printed box at Q509 spells out: *\"Reduced red reflex or yellowish reflex (if the vitreous cavity is full of pus\"*. Pus is pale and scatters light; blood is dark and swallows it.\n- **\"B&C\"** is wrong because **C is wrong**. It is the option that catches anyone who reasons *\"anything filling the vitreous kills the reflex\"* without asking what colour the filling is. The module's whole point in slides 51–52 is that **the colour of the abnormal reflex names the disease**, and it draws the line between an *absent/black* reflex and a *yellow/white* one deliberately.\n\n**In the photograph itself,** one pupil glows orange-red with a normal reflex while the fellow pupil is uniformly black with only the corneal flash on it — the asymmetry that makes the abnormal side obvious. That asymmetric comparison is the reason the red reflex is tested **binocularly and in a darkened room**, and the reason `opmcq-c1-35` in this corpus is built as a matching exercise across all four colours.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A black or absent red reflex means a dense vitreous haemorrhage (or a mature cataract); retinal detachment gives a grey reflex and endophthalmitis a yellow-white one, so the colour of the reflex names the disease.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.79 (Q501, with figure), key p.81' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-502', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'A **65-year-old highly myopic woman** presents with sudden onset of **“flying flies”** and **intermittent flashes of light for 2 days**. **VA is normal.** What is the most likely diagnosis?',
    options:['Iritis','Uveitis','Posterior vitreous detachment','Central retinal artery occlusion'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Symptoms of PVD:**\n\n**1. Floaters:** the patient complains of seeing \"bugs\", \"cobwebs\", or \"spots\" due to vitreous condensations that move with eye movements appearing especially in bright light. Although most floaters are benign, new, or markedly increased floaters or flashes of light require a dilated fundus exam to rule out Retinal tears/detachment,\n\n**2. Flashes of light (Photopsia):** due to traction on the retina by the vitreous base\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Three details in the stem all point the same way, and none of them is the floaters alone.**\n\n| Stem detail | What it rules in or out |\n|---|---|\n| **\"Flying flies\"** | *Muscae volitantes* — the classical description of **floaters**. `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 82 defines the symptom as *\"Seeing floating black shadows in bright illumination\"*, and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42 lists **\"Posterior vitreous detachment (PVD)\"** first among its causes. |\n| **Flashes of light** | **Photopsia** — `L1,2` slide 82, *\"Seeing flashes of light in the absence of a light source\"*. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 lists **PVD** first among its three causes (PVD · retinal tear/detachment · migraine with aura). |\n| **Highly myopic, 65** | The two risk factors that bring the vitreous off early. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 6 names **\"Aging (vitreous shrinkage)\"** and **\"high myopia\"** together; `L7,8) Refractive errors` slide 46 lists retinal tears and RRD among the peripheral complications of myopia. |\n| **VA is normal** | The macula is untouched, so nothing has detached **through the fovea** and no artery has closed. This is the detail that separates a benign PVD from the things it can lead to. |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n- **Central retinal artery occlusion** is the easiest to exclude and the easiest to misread as \"sudden\". CRAO is **sudden, painless, profound loss of vision** — a normal acuity excludes it outright, and it produces neither floaters nor flashes.\n- **Iritis** and **Uveitis** are inflammatory, and this eye has no inflammatory symptom at all: no pain, no redness, no photophobia, no drop in vision. `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 16 gives the anterior-uveitis signs as **\"Cells & flare ± hypopyon\"** in a painful photophobic eye. Floaters *can* occur in **intermediate and posterior** uveitis — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42 lists both among the causes of floaters, and `opqb-t10-430` in this corpus is a vitritis vignette — but they come with **blurred vision**, which this patient does not have.\n- **⚠️ A printed defect worth naming: options A and B overlap.** Iritis *is* an anterior uveitis, so A is a subset of B and the two can never be distinguished from one another. Nothing turns on it here because both are wrong, but it is the same shape as `opqb-t3-127`. The key is not disputed.\n\n**⚠️ The clinically important point is not the diagnosis but what follows it,** and the bank's own box says it: new or markedly increased floaters or flashes **require a dilated fundus exam to rule out retinal tears or detachment**. That is what Q504 keys.",
    objective:'New floaters and flashes with a normal acuity in an elderly myope is a posterior vitreous detachment; the finding obliges a dilated peripheral examination to exclude a retinal tear.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.79 (Q502), key p.81 (printed explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-503', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'In the previous question — the **65-year-old highly myopic woman** with two days of **“flying flies”** and intermittent **flashes of light** and a **normal VA** — what finding on **fundus exam** is characteristic of this condition?',
    options:['Cherry-red spot','Weiss ring','Flame hemorrhages','Drusen'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Ophthalmoscopy:**\n\n**Weiss ring:** a ring formed by the glial tissue around the optic disc that remains attached to the detached posterior vitreous\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**What you are looking at, and why it is a ring.** The vitreous is anchored most firmly at three places, and one of them is the rim of the optic disc. When the gel peels backwards it tears that annular attachment away with it, so a ring of glial tissue comes free and floats in the vitreous just in front of the disc — visible with the ophthalmoscope as a mobile grey-white circle, and visible **to the patient** as the single large floater that so often brings them in. *(The three-point anchorage and the patient's-eye view are **not taken from the course material**; the ring itself, and its origin at the disc margin, are the bank's own printed box.)*\n\n**Why it settles the diagnosis:** the ring can only exist if the posterior hyaloid has separated. It is therefore not merely *consistent with* a posterior vitreous detachment — it is the **direct sign of one**, which is why the bank calls it *characteristic*.\n\n**Why the other three belong to other diseases:**\n\n| Option | Whose sign it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Cherry-red spot** | **Central retinal artery occlusion.** The whole inner retina goes white with oedema while the fovea, thin enough to show the choroidal circulation through it, stays red by contrast. This corpus keys it as the CRAO/CRVO discriminator at `opqb-t13-514`, where *cherry red spot* is the printed **EXCEPT** in a list of vein-occlusion findings. |\n| **Flame hemorrhages** | **Central retinal vein occlusion** (and hypertensive retinopathy). Superficial haemorrhages track along the nerve fibre layer and take its feathered shape; `L19.2) Retinal Vascular Disorders` gives CRVO its scattered haemorrhages in all four quadrants. |\n| **Drusen** | **Age-related macular degeneration** — yellowish sub-RPE deposits at the posterior pole. `L20`'s own NOT-COVERED list records that AMD is absent from that deck and that *\"Drusen appears ONLY as an unexplained label on the slide-12 exudative fundus\"*, so the association is stated here from general knowledge and tagged *(not taken from the course material)*. Note that drusen are **static deposits at the macula**, not a mobile opacity in the vitreous, and they do not cause floaters. |\n\n**⚠️ The Weiss ring is not in the lecture decks.** `L20`'s closing negatives list states it explicitly — *\"POSTERIOR VITREOUS DETACHMENT (PVD) as a named entity, and Weiss ring\"* are not covered — and grepping `weiss` case-insensitively across all 29 lecture caches and all 12 cached book chapters returns **exactly one hit, and that hit is the negatives list itself**. The term reaches this corpus only through this bank's printed box, which is why the box above is the primary source for the entry.",
    objective:'A Weiss ring — the glial annulus torn from the disc margin and floating in front of it — is the direct ophthalmoscopic sign of posterior vitreous detachment.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.79 (Q503), key p.81 (printed explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-504', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'In the previous question — the **65-year-old highly myopic woman** with two days of **“flying flies”** and intermittent **flashes of light** and a **normal VA** — what is the **most important next step**?',
    options:['Start topical steroids','Order CT orbit','Dilated fundus exam to rule out retinal tear','Reassure and discharge'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The bank answers this one in its own words two questions earlier.** The printed box at `opqb-t12-502` states: *\"Although most floaters are benign, new, or markedly increased floaters or flashes of light **require a dilated fundus exam to rule out Retinal tears/detachment**\"* — the option is the sentence.\n\n**Why it is the *most important* step and not merely a reasonable one.** A posterior vitreous detachment is itself harmless. What is not harmless is the minority in which the separating gel, where it is stuck hardest, **tears the retina instead of letting go of it** — and a tear left alone lets liquefied vitreous through into the subretinal space, which is `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 6's mechanism for a rhegmatogenous detachment: *\"Vitreous fluid … seeps through the tear and collects in the subretinal space, physically pushing the retina away from its underlying blood supply.\"* A tear found today is closed with **laser photocoagulation** in an outpatient sitting — this bank keys exactly that at `opqb-t13-571`, in a high myope with a tear and no detachment. The same tear found in a fortnight is a theatre case.\n\n**And the tear will not be where you are looking.** Breaks are **peripheral**, so the examination has to be a **dilated** one, reaching the far retina with an indirect ophthalmoscope or a three-mirror lens. `L18) Cataract II` slide 20 prescribes the same sweep for any fundus examination — *\"Periphery: retinal breaks, lattice degeneration\"* — and `opqb-t13-536` in this corpus names the **Goldmann 3 mirror lens** as the way to reach it. An undilated look at the posterior pole is how a peripheral horseshoe tear gets missed, and `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 53 states the reason plainly: *\"Pharmacologic dilation of the patient's pupils greatly facilitates ophthalmoscopy.\"*\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Reassure and discharge** | The commonest real-world error, and the one this question exists to punish. The reassurance is only earned **after** the periphery has been seen; giving it first is discharging an untreated retinal break. |\n| **Start topical steroids** | Treats inflammation. There is none: no pain, no redness, no photophobia, no cells. Steroids here achieve nothing and carry their own risks of raised intraocular pressure and cataract. |\n| **Order CT orbit** | Images bone, orbital soft tissue and gross globe pathology. It cannot resolve a retinal break, and it delays the examination that can. `L25) Trauma I` slide 52 reserves CT for a suspected **intraocular foreign body**, which is not this patient. |\n\n**The rule:** flashes and floaters are a **symptom of traction**, and traction is a reason to look at the retina today. `opqb-t13-569` and `opqb-t13-558` in this corpus key the identical instinct — the answer to new photopsia is **fundus examination**, not an investigation and not a follow-up appointment.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'New flashes and floaters oblige a dilated peripheral fundus examination the same day, because the treatable lesion is a peripheral retinal tear and laser closes it before it detaches.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.79 (Q504), key p.81' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-505', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret-dr',
    stem:'A **58-year-old diabetic** patient reports **sudden, painless loss of vision followed by complete blackness**. **Fundus view is obscured.** What is the most likely cause in this patient?',
    options:['PVD','Proliferative diabetic retinopathy','Optic neuritis','Cataract'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**\"Fundus view is obscured\" is the whole question.** Something opaque has appeared between the examiner's light and the retina, in a diabetic, overnight. In this patient that is **blood in the vitreous cavity, bled from the new vessels of proliferative diabetic retinopathy.**\n\n**The chain the module teaches, link by link:**\n\n| Link | Source |\n|---|---|\n| Diabetes progresses from non-proliferative to **proliferative** disease, in which **new vessels** grow on the disc (**NVD**) or elsewhere (**NVE**) | `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy`: *\"Proliferative diabetic retinopathy · Affects 5-10% of diabetics · IDD at increased risk (60% after 30 years) · Neovascularization · Flat or elevated\"* |\n| Those vessels bleed into the vitreous | ⚠️ `L19.1` never says so in prose, but it prints the consequence as its **first indication for vitreoretinal surgery**: *\"**Severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage**\"*, with *\"Dense, persistent premacular haemorrhage\"* beside it. The vessels are fragile, unsupported and adherent to a vitreous that moves *(the fragility is **not taken from the course material**)*. |\n| A vitreous haemorrhage presents as **acute painless loss of vision** | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 lists **\"1. Vitreous haemorrhage\"** at the head of its *Acute painless loss of vision* box; `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 133 puts **\"Vitreous Haemorrhage\"** in the *Acute* column of its loss-of-vision table. |\n| And it blocks the view in | `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 50: *\"Opacities in the media (e.g., corneal scar, cataract, **vitreous haemorrhage**) appear as black shadows\"*; slide 51 gives a **dense** vitreous haemorrhage an **absent (black) reflex**. |\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **PVD** | ⚠️ **The deliberate trap, because Q502 three numbers earlier keys exactly this option.** A posterior vitreous detachment gives **floaters and flashes with normal acuity** and leaves the media clear — the bank's own box at `opqb-t12-502` says most floaters are benign. It does not black out vision and it does not obscure the fundus. The two questions are a matched pair: same organ, opposite severity. |\n| **Optic neuritis** | The media stay **crystal clear** in optic neuritis — the lesion is behind the eye, so the examiner's view of the fundus is unobstructed (the disc may even look entirely normal in the retrobulbar form). It also typically strikes a **young adult** and hurts **on eye movement**, neither of which fits. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 lists it under acute painless loss, but with a normal or swollen disc that you can see. |\n| **Cataract** | Wrong **tempo**. Cataract opacifies the media, and `L1,2` slide 133 duly files it under **Chronic** visual loss — months to years of progressive blur, not a sudden blackout. `L17) Cataract I` and `L18) Cataract II` describe a **gradual** painless drop throughout. A diabetic certainly cataracts earlier, but not between one hour and the next. |\n\n**⚠️ Note what the stem does not say:** there is no pain, no redness and no trauma. That triad of absences, plus an obscured fundus in a long-standing diabetic, is the fingerprint of vitreous haemorrhage.\n\n*(Secondary chapter: `op-acute` — the vignette is a presentation of acute painless visual loss, and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 is that chapter's list; it is filed under diabetic retinopathy because the option the bank keys is the **disease**, not the symptom.)*\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Sudden painless blackout with an obscured fundus in a long-standing diabetic is a vitreous haemorrhage from proliferative diabetic retinopathy; PVD leaves the media clear, optic neuritis leaves them clear and hurts, and cataract is chronic.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.79 (Q505), key p.81' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-506', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'In the previous question — the **58-year-old diabetic** with **sudden, painless loss of vision followed by complete blackness** and an **obscured fundus view** — what would you expect on **ophthalmoscopy**?',
    options:['Cherry-red spot','Cup-to-disc enlargement','Black shadows against red reflex','Cotton-wool spots only'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**`L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 50 is the answer, almost word for word:**\n\n> *\"Opacities in the media (e.g., corneal scar, cataract, **vitreous haemorrhage**) appear as **black shadows** and can be best seen when the pupil has been dilated.\"*\n\n**Why blood looks like this and not like anything else.** The red reflex is light that has gone in, bounced off the fundus and come back out (`L3,4` slide 49: *\"Normal red reflex is not interrupted by shadows, and is evidence that the cornea, anterior chamber, lens, and vitreous are clear\"*). Blood suspended in the gel absorbs that light on both journeys, so wherever a clot or a sheet of red cells sits, the returning glow is missing — a **dark silhouette against whatever red reflex survives around it**. As the haemorrhage thickens, the shadows coalesce until nothing comes back at all, and slide 51 gives that end state its own name: **\"Dense vitreous hemorrhage (Black reflex)\"** — which is what `opqb-t12-501` keys.\n\n**Why the other three belong to other diseases:**\n\n| Option | Whose sign it is, and why it cannot be seen here |\n|---|---|\n| **Cherry-red spot** | **Central retinal artery occlusion.** Beyond being the wrong disease, it is a **retinal** sign — you have to be able to see the retina to find it, and the stem has already said the fundus view is obscured. |\n| **Cup-to-disc enlargement** | **Glaucomatous optic neuropathy** — a slow, painless, chronic loss of field, not a sudden blackout. Again, a disc sign requires a visible disc. |\n| **Cotton-wool spots only** | Cotton-wool spots are real in diabetes — `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` lists them among the signs of **severe NPDR** alongside dark blot haemorrhages, venous irregularities and IRMA. ⚠️ **The word that makes the option wrong is \"only\".** Cotton-wool spots are a background finding that does not obscure the media and does not cause a sudden blackout; keying them would mean the vitreous is clear, which contradicts the stem. |\n\n**⚠️ The examination point the question is really making:** when the media are opaque, **stop looking for retinal signs and start reading the reflex itself.** The colour and pattern of what comes back — black shadows, absent black, grey, yellow-white — is the only information the eye is still giving you, and `L3,4` slides 50–52 turn that into a diagnosis. `opmcq-c1-35` in this corpus tests the same four-way mapping directly.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Blood in the vitreous absorbs the returning light, so it appears as black shadows interrupting the red reflex — and once dense, as an absent black reflex; retinal and disc signs cannot be read through opaque media at all.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.79 (Q506), key p.81' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-507', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'In the previous question — the **58-year-old diabetic** with **sudden, painless loss of vision** and an **obscured fundus view** — what investigation is **essential to rule out retinal detachment**?',
    options:['OCT','CT orbit','B-scan ultrasound','MRI'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**B-scan ultrasound is the test for an eye you cannot look into, and the module says so twice in the same lecture.**\n\n> `L18) Cataract II` slide 12, *Posterior Segment Evaluation*: **\"B-scan ultrasound if fundus not visible → rule out retinal detachment / tumour.\"**\n>\n> `L18` slide 20, *Fundal Examination*: **\"If not visible → B-scan ultrasound mandatory.\"**\n\nThe first of those two lines is this question with the words rearranged. Sound does not care that the vitreous is full of blood: it crosses the opaque media, reflects off the retina, and draws its position and contour on the screen. `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 37 uses the same instrument for the same reason — *\"U/S: Scleral thickening ± RD\"* — and `L24) Ocular tumors.` lists **\"Ocular U/S (B-Scan)\"** among the investigations for an intraocular mass.\n\n**Why this is not optional.** A vitreous haemorrhage and a rhegmatogenous detachment can present identically when you cannot see in, and they are managed completely differently: blood alone may be observed and may clear, while a detachment — particularly a **tractional** one in a diabetic, which `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 15 treats with **vitrectomy and membrane peeling** — is a surgical decision that cannot wait for the blood to settle. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` lists **\"Retinal detachment involving macula\"** among its four indications for vitreoretinal surgery. The scan is what tells you which of the two you are dealing with.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **OCT** | Optical coherence tomography is **optical** — it uses light, so it is defeated by exactly the media opacity that created the problem. `L18` slide 12 places it correctly in the same list, but hedged: *\"OCT **when possible** in hypermature cataract.\"* Excellent for the macula in a clear eye, useless through dense blood. |\n| **CT orbit** | Images bone and orbital soft tissue. Its ophthalmic indication in this module is a suspected **intraocular foreign body** — `L25) Trauma I` slide 52, *\"CT SCAN – Determine the location and presence of a metallic IOFB\"* — and orbital pathology. It does not have the resolution to show a detached retina reliably, and it irradiates the lens. |\n| **MRI** | Slower, more expensive, no better at the retina, and `L25` slide 52 prints the one absolute rule attached to it: **\"DO NOT DO MRI SCAN IF A METALLIC IOFB IS SUSPECTED.\"** |\n\n**⚠️ The general rule, worth separating from this vignette:** ultrasound's indication is **opaque media**, whatever the cause — dense cataract, vitreous haemorrhage, corneal scar. `opqb-t9-371` in this corpus asks the same thing behind a mature cataract, and `opqb-t13-536` marks *\"Ultrasonography must be done\"* as the **false** statement precisely because that patient's *\"fundus could be seen clearly\"*. Clear media, look; opaque media, scan.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'When the media are opaque, B-scan ultrasound is the only way to assess the retina — light-based tests such as OCT are defeated by the same opacity, and CT and MRI image the orbit rather than the retina.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.80 (Q507), key p.81' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-508', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'A **70-year-old man** presents **3 days after cataract surgery** with **severe eye pain, redness, photophobia and marked vision drop to PL**. What is the most likely diagnosis?',
    options:['Acute angle-closure glaucoma','Endophthalmitis','Optic neuritis','Scleritis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Etiology of Endophthalmitis and Vitritis**\n\n**A. Exogenous:**\n\n**1.** As postoperative complication: Most common cause **[the risk following cataract surgery is less than 0.1%].**\n\n**2.** Penetrating injury to the eye (risk is 3-7%).\n\n(Remember to ask about tetanus vaccination in post -traumatic endophthalmitis)\n\n**3.** Intravitreal injections.\n\n**B. Endogenous spread.**\n\n- Infectious organisms are mostly bacterial but may be fungal.\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim, including its \"post -traumatic\" spacing. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The timing is the diagnosis.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 16 tabulates the postoperative complications by **when** they appear, and endophthalmitis occupies the earliest slot:\n\n| Complication | Onset (`L18` slide 16) |\n|---|---|\n| **Endophthalmitis** | **24–72 hrs (acute)** or weeks (delayed) — *\"Most serious vision-threatening complication.\"* |\n| Cystoid macular oedema | Weeks–months |\n| Posterior capsule opacification | Months–years |\n\nThree days is inside the acute window, and the deck gives the exact clinical picture the stem lists: **\"Signs: pain, redness, reduced VA, hypopyon, yellowish vitreous haze.\"** Its own rapid-fire revision slide (slide 21) asks *\"Most serious post-op complication?\"* and answers *\"Endophthalmitis.\"* The organisms are named too: **\"CoNS (Staphylococcus epidermidis most common), S. aureus, streptococci.\"**\n\n**\"Vision drop to PL\" is the alarm.** Perception of light only, three days after routine surgery that should be improving daily, is a collapse — not a slow recovery. Any pain and vision loss in the first week after intraocular surgery is endophthalmitis until proven otherwise, because the treatment window is measured in hours.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute angle-closure glaucoma** | Pain, redness and dropped vision fit, but the mechanism does not: this eye has just had its **lens removed**, and a pseudophakic eye has a deep anterior chamber and a wide angle. `L18` teaches that the growing crystalline lens is what crowds the angle in the first place, and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.17 names lens growth as the involutional cause of angle-closure. AACG also brings **coloured haloes, a mid-dilated fixed pupil, a stony-hard globe and vomiting** — none of them in the stem. |\n| **Optic neuritis** | Painful — but **on eye movement**, not a severe constant ache — and the eye is **white and quiet**: no redness, no photophobia. It is also a disease of young adults, not of a 70-year-old three days after surgery. |\n| **Scleritis** | Genuinely severe, boring pain that wakes the patient, with a deep violaceous injection. But it is not a postoperative-day-three event, it is usually associated with systemic connective-tissue disease, and it does not by itself drop vision to perception of light. |\n\n**⚠️ Note the corpus already carries the same scenario from the other bank direction:** `opqb-t9-355` puts the same patient **48 hours** after extraction with *\"ciliary injection, corneal oedema and absent red reflex\"* and keys **bacterial endophthalmitis** from a different option list.",
    objective:'Pain, redness and a collapse of vision within 24–72 hours of cataract surgery is acute postoperative endophthalmitis — the most serious complication of the operation and the one with the shortest treatment window.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.80 (Q508), key p.81 (printed explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-509', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'In the previous question — the **70-year-old man 3 days after cataract surgery** with **severe pain, redness, photophobia and vision down to PL** — which finding is most associated with that condition?',
    options:['Weiss ring','Hypopyon','Drusen','Kayser-Fleischer ring'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Ophthalmoscopy of Endophthalmitis:**\n\n**- Reduced red reflex or yellowish reflex (if the vitreous cavity is full of pus**\n\n- Associated signs of a ruptured globe may be present e.g., severe subconjunctival hemorrhage, hyphema, decreased IOP, etc\n\n---\n\n*(The lines above are the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim. ⚠️ **The parenthesis opened at \"(if the vitreous cavity is full of pus\" is never closed in the source** — checked at 300 dpi; the line simply stops. Recorded, not corrected. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**Hypopyon is pus in the anterior chamber, and it is the sign the module attaches to this diagnosis by name.** `L18) Cataract II` slide 16 lists the signs of postoperative endophthalmitis as **\"pain, redness, reduced VA, hypopyon, yellowish vitreous haze\"** — hypopyon is on the list, in that order. `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 27 defines what you are seeing at the slit lamp: *\"Blood (Hyphema) | Pus (Hypopyon) | Cells & Flare (Uveitis)\"*, and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 photographs it as *\"a white-yellow fluid level below a hazy cornea\"* while listing **endophthalmitis** among the causes of a red eye on the same page.\n\n**Why pus settles into a level.** Leucocytes pouring into an inflamed eye are heavier than aqueous and sediment under gravity, so they form a flat, mobile, creamy layer at the bottom of the anterior chamber — flat-topped because it is a fluid level, and it shifts if the patient's head is tipped *(the physical explanation is **not taken from the course material**; the sign and its meaning are)*.\n\n**⚠️ Hypopyon is not specific to endophthalmitis, and the corpus records the alternatives.** It also appears in severe **anterior uveitis** — `L13,14) Uvea & Sclera` slide 16, *\"Anterior chamber: Cells & flare ± hypopyon\"* — and in a **hypopyon corneal ulcer**, which `opqb-t8-306` keys to pneumococcus. What makes it point at endophthalmitis here is the **context the stem supplies**: three days after intraocular surgery, with pain and vision at perception of light. `opqb-t10-445` in this corpus makes the same discrimination from the uveitis side, keying *\"a tender globe together with a hypopyon highly suggest an infectious origin\"* as a **true** statement.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong — each belongs to a disease elsewhere in this very topic:**\n\n| Option | Whose sign it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Weiss ring** | **Posterior vitreous detachment** — the glial ring torn from the disc margin, keyed four questions earlier at `opqb-t12-503`. A floater, not an inflammatory sign. |\n| **Drusen** | Yellowish sub-RPE deposits of **age-related macular degeneration** — painless, chronic, at the macula *(the association is **not taken from the course material**; `L20`'s negatives list records that AMD is not covered in that deck)*. |\n| **Kayser-Fleischer ring** | **Wilson's disease** — a ring of copper deposited in **Descemet's membrane** at the corneal periphery. Systemic, chronic, and nothing to do with the anterior chamber contents. |",
    objective:'Hypopyon — a settled layer of pus in the anterior chamber — is the classic anterior sign of postoperative endophthalmitis, alongside a reduced or yellowish red reflex from pus in the vitreous.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.80 (Q509), key p.81 (printed explanation box)' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-510', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-cat-surg',
    stem:'In the previous question — the **70-year-old man 3 days after cataract surgery** with **severe pain, redness, photophobia and vision down to PL** — what is the **best immediate management**?',
    options:['Topical steroids only','Immediate intravitreal antibiotics ± vitrectomy','Oral doxycycline','Eye patch'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**`L18) Cataract II` slide 16 prints the answer as two clauses, and the printed option is both of them:**\n\n> **\"Tx: urgent vitreous tap + intravitreal antibiotics (vancomycin + ceftazidime). Severe: vitrectomy.\"**\n\nIts revision slide (slide 21) repeats the drugs: *\"Most serious post-op complication? — **Endophthalmitis — intravitreal vancomycin + ceftazidime**.\"*\n\n**Why the antibiotic must go INTO the vitreous.** The blood–ocular barrier keeps systemically administered drugs out of the vitreous cavity at anything like a bactericidal concentration, and the vitreous is avascular gel — there is no circulation to carry a tablet there. Injecting through the pars plana puts the drug where the organisms are, in a single therapeutic dose *(the barrier and the pharmacokinetics are **not taken from the course material**; the route and the drugs are `L18` slide 16)*.\n\n**Why the two named drugs.** **Vancomycin** covers the Gram-positive organisms the deck lists as the usual culprits — *\"CoNS (Staphylococcus epidermidis most common), S. aureus, streptococci\"* — and **ceftazidime** covers Gram-negatives including *Pseudomonas*. Together they are empirical cover started before any culture returns, which is the point: the **vitreous tap is taken and the antibiotic given in the same sitting**, sample first, treatment immediately after.\n\n**And why \"± vitrectomy\".** The deck's own qualifier is **\"Severe: vitrectomy\"** — removing the infected gel takes out the bulk of the organisms, the toxins and the inflammatory debris at once. It is the same operation as in `opqb-t12-498` and `opqb-t12-499`, indicated here because the vitreous itself has become the problem.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Topical steroids only** | ⚠️ **The dangerous answer, and the word is \"only\".** Steroids do have a place *alongside* antibiotic cover, to limit the inflammatory destruction of the retina. Given **alone** in a bacterial endophthalmitis they suppress the host response while the organisms multiply unopposed. |\n| **Oral doxycycline** | A systemic tetracycline, and the wrong drug by both route and spectrum. Its ophthalmic role in this module is chronic **blepharitis and meibomian gland disease**, not an intraocular infection. It will not reach the vitreous in useful concentration. |\n| **Eye patch** | Does nothing to an infection. Patching has its indications — a corneal abrasion, a post-operative dressing — and delay is the one thing this diagnosis cannot survive. |\n\n**⚠️ The single sentence worth remembering:** endophthalmitis is diagnosed clinically and treated the same hour — **tap and inject** — because the retina is being destroyed while the decision is being made.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Acute postoperative endophthalmitis is treated by immediate vitreous tap and intravitreal vancomycin plus ceftazidime, with vitrectomy in severe cases; topical steroids alone or oral antibiotics do not reach or control it.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.80 (Q510), key p.81' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-511', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-ret',
    stem:'**Flashes of light in PVD** are due to:',
    options:['Inflammation','Damage to optic nerve','Traction on retina','Myopic error'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The bank answers this in its own printed box nine questions earlier.** `opqb-t12-502` prints: *\"**2. Flashes of light (Photopsia):** due to **traction on the retina by the vitreous base**.\"* The option is the box.\n\n**Why traction produces light and not, say, pain.** The retina has one output. Whatever stimulus reaches a photoreceptor or a bipolar cell — photons, pressure, a tug — the signal that leaves the eye is indistinguishable from light, so the brain reports a flash. `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 82 defines the symptom precisely on that basis: **photopsia is \"seeing flashes of light in the absence of a light source\"**. *(That the retina has no pain fibres and encodes every stimulus as light is **not taken from the course material** — the corpus states it at `opqb-t13-565`, where this bank's own keyed option reads \"Stimulation of photoreceptors by the tear since the retina has no sensitivity to pain and only sensitive to light\".)*\n\n**Where the pull comes from.** As the vitreous liquefies and peels backwards it stays firmly anchored at the **vitreous base**, the ring of adhesion straddling the ora serrata. Every eye movement then jerks the still-attached gel against that anchorage, which is why the flashes are **brief, recurrent, typically in the temporal periphery, and worse in the dark and on moving the eye**. `L20) Retinal detachment and degenerations` slide 10 names the same force from the pathological side — *\"Commonly caused by **vitreous traction** leading to tears\"* — and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.41 lists **PVD** and **retinal tear/detachment** as the first two causes of photopsia, one after the other, because they are the same mechanism at two severities.\n\n**Why the other three are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Inflammation** | Uveitis and vitritis produce **floaters** from inflammatory cells in the gel (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.42 lists intermediate and posterior uveitis among the causes of floaters) — but cells drift, they do not pull. There is no traction and therefore no photopsia, and the eye is painful and photophobic, which a PVD is not. |\n| **Damage to optic nerve** | Optic nerve disease subtracts vision rather than adding sensation: a **field defect**, a **central scotoma**, dyschromatopsia, an afferent pupillary defect. `opqb-t13-565` prints \"axoplasmic transport delay in the optic nerve\" as a distractor for the same reason. |\n| **Myopic error** | Refraction blurs the image; it does not generate one. Myopia is a **risk factor** for PVD because the long globe liquefies its gel early (`L20` slide 6, *\"high myopia\"* as an RRD cause; `L7,8) Refractive errors` slide 46), but the refractive error is not itself the source of the flash. |\n\n**⚠️ Clinically:** flashes mean something is pulling on the retina **now**, which is why `opqb-t12-504` keys a dilated examination and not reassurance.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Photopsia in PVD comes from mechanical traction of the still-adherent vitreous on the retina at the vitreous base — the retina reports every stimulus, mechanical included, as light.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.80 (Q511), key p.81' },

  { id:'opqb-t12-512', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'**Yellowish reflex** in fundus is characteristic of:',
    options:['Macular edema','Endophthalmitis','Retinal vein occlusion','Optic neuritis'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**`L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` slide 52 gives the yellow reflex two owners, and one of them is on the option list:**\n\n> **\"Yellow/ white Reflex: – Retinoblastoma – Pus in the vitreous (endophthalmitis)\"**\n\nThis bank's own printed box at `opqb-t12-509` says the same thing from the ophthalmoscope's side: *\"Reduced red reflex or yellowish reflex (if the vitreous cavity is full of pus\"*, and `L18) Cataract II` slide 16 lists **\"yellowish vitreous haze\"** among the signs of postoperative endophthalmitis. Three independent sources, one colour.\n\n**Why pus is yellow where blood is black.** The red reflex is light returning from the fundus. **Blood absorbs** it, so a dense vitreous haemorrhage gives a **black** reflex — the key at `opqb-t12-501`. **Pus scatters** it: a suspension of leucocytes and fibrin bounces the light back before it ever reaches the retina, and what comes out is the pale creamy-yellow colour of the exudate itself. The same physics gives retinoblastoma its white reflex — a solid pale tumour reflecting the light forward.\n\n**The four-colour key this topic keeps returning to** (`L3,4` slides 50–52):\n\n| Reflex | Cause |\n|---|---|\n| Black shadows in a red reflex | Media opacities — corneal scar, cataract, vitreous haemorrhage |\n| **Absent / black** | Mature cataract · dense vitreous haemorrhage |\n| **Grey** | Retinal detachment |\n| **Yellow / white** | Retinoblastoma · **pus in the vitreous (endophthalmitis)** |\n\n**Why the other three cannot change the colour of the reflex at all:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Macular edema** | Retinal thickening at the fovea, a few disc diameters across. `L19.1) Diabetic Retinopathy` defines clinically significant macular oedema by millimetre distances from the foveal centre — a lesion far too small and far too transparent to alter the reflex of the whole pupil. It blurs and distorts central vision; you find it with a slit lamp and OCT, not with a reflex. |\n| **Retinal vein occlusion** | Produces **haemorrhage** — scattered flame and blot haemorrhages, dilated tortuous veins, disc oedema (`opqb-t13-514`). If it altered the reflex at all it would **darken** it, and only if it bled into the vitreous. |\n| **Optic neuritis** | The media are clear and the reflex is **normal**. The abnormality is functional — reduced acuity, a central scotoma, an afferent pupillary defect, pain on eye movement — with a disc that is either swollen or, in the retrobulbar form, entirely unremarkable. |\n\n**⚠️ Do not read this key as \"yellow reflex = endophthalmitis\" alone.** In a **child**, a white or yellow pupillary reflex is **retinoblastoma until proven otherwise** — the same slide names it first, and `opqb-t9-356` in this corpus tests leukocoria directly. The option list here simply contains no paediatric alternative.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A yellowish or white pupillary reflex means light is being scattered back before it reaches the retina — pus in the vitreous (endophthalmitis) or a retinoblastoma; blood gives a black reflex and detachment a grey one.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.80 (Q512), key p.81' }
